 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Account for the actual byte size of each cached JPEG; temporary render output and replacement metadata do not count toward this budget. Temporary output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage, removes pending jobs, or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Two-Queue Replacement
+
+Use 2Q with two queues of resident cache entries:
+
+- **A1in** is a FIFO probationary queue. Admit a newly rendered sheet at its tail after the first completed render. A cache hit in A1in removes the entry and promotes it to the most-recently-used end of Am.
+- **Am** is an LRU queue for sheets that have been requested at least twice while resident. A cache hit in Am moves the entry to its most-recently-used end.
+
+Only resident sheets occupy either queue. Pending jobs and temporary output have no queue entries, and eviction removes both the JPEG and its queue metadata without retaining a non-resident history entry. A cache lookup therefore changes queue order but never creates a render job; a miss follows the ordinary rendering and deduplication flow.
+
+### Capacity and Eviction
+
+Reserve 25% of the cache budget (3 GiB) for A1in and 75% (9 GiB) for Am. These are independent byte limits whose sum is the 12 GiB cache budget; unused space in one queue does not enlarge the other.
+
+Before admitting a new sheet to A1in, evict sheets from its FIFO head until A1in can hold the new sheet within 3 GiB. Before promoting a sheet to Am, evict sheets from Am's LRU end until Am can hold the promoted sheet within 9 GiB. Promotion itself does not change total cached JPEG bytes. Maintaining both queue limits keeps their combined occupancy within 12 GiB.
+
+A sheet larger than the 3 GiB A1in limit is returned to its requester but is not admitted to the cache. Render completion for a key that is already cached neither refreshes the bytes nor creates a duplicate entry; it follows the normal hit transition for the queue currently holding that key.
+
+All cache lookup, admission, promotion, and eviction transitions are serialized within the single preview worker so queue membership and byte accounting agree with the files on disk.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|-------------------------|-----------|
+| Resident-cache replacement | 2Q with FIFO A1in and LRU Am | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Separating first-use sheets from reused sheets protects comparison sheets during sequential screenings without retaining stale frequency counters. The additional queues and size tuning remain private to the preview worker. |
+| Initial queue-size split | 3 GiB A1in and 9 GiB Am | A larger probationary share; dynamically shared capacity | A small probationary queue limits scan displacement while leaving most capacity for reused sheets. Fixed independent limits make admission and promotion accounting deterministic; workload measurements can later justify retuning the split. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. Use 2Q to favor repeatedly used comparison sheets over one-off screening sheets.
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Retune the A1in/Am split if observed promotion and eviction behavior shows that the initial 25%/75% division poorly serves production workloads.
